 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Popping Artifacts:** Using fewer Gaussians results in larger Gaussians on average, which increases the prevalence of noticeable popping artifacts [S4].
-*   **View-Dependent Colors:** Enabling SH coefficients during densification provides minimal benefit and wastes resources; they should be disabled until after simplification [S2].
-*   **Precision:** The method relies on accurate depth cues for reinitialization; errors in depth estimation can lead to poor spatial distribution [S2].
+*   **Popping Artifacts:** Using fewer Gaussians results in larger Gaussians on average, which increases the prevalence of noticeable popping artifacts during camera movement [S4].
+*   **SH Redundancy:** View-dependent colors (SH coefficients) provide minimal benefit during the densification phase; enabling them early wastes resources without improving spatial distribution [S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Model Compression Pipeline for 3D Gaussian Splatting](9987-model-compression-pipeline-for-3d-gaussian-splatting.md)
-This method is a compression and rendering pipeline for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that reduces memory footprint and increases rendering speed while maintaining image quality. It addresses the high memory bandwidth and computational complexity of standard 3DGS, making it suitable for edge deployment, mobile VR/AR, and low-power hardware. It sets itself apart from Mini-Splatting by not imposing a hard limit on the total number of Gaussians via mixed-integer optimization, but rather by iteratively pruning, quantizing attributes, and reducing spherical harmonic (SH) degrees to achieve high compression ratios (up to 51.6×) with minimal quality loss. It differs from LightGaussian by using iterative pruning with intermediate fine-tuning, progressive SH-degree reduction, and vector quantization (VQ) on all SH coefficients and colors, rather than only low-salience subsets.
+This method is a compression and rendering pipeline for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that reduces memory footprint and increases rendering speed while maintaining image quality. It addresses the high memory bandwidth and computational complexity of standard 3DGS, making it suitable for edge deployment, mobile VR/AR, and low-power hardware. It sets itself apart from its parent [Mini-Splatting](7850-mini-splatting.md) by not imposing a hard limit on the total number of Gaussians via mixed-integer optimization, but rather by iteratively pruning, quantizing attributes, and reducing spherical harmonic (SH) degrees to achieve high compression ratios (up to 51.6×) with minimal quality loss. It differs from LightGaussian by using iterative pruning with intermediate fine-tuning, progressive SH-degree reduction, and vector quantization (VQ) on all SH coefficients and colors, rather than only low-salience subsets.
 
 ### Mini-Splatting-D
-A quality-prioritized variant of Mini-Splatting that omits the simplification process to maximize rendering quality [S2]. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), Mini-Splatting-D uses 4.69M Gaussians, achieving SSIM 0.831, PSNR 27.51, LPIPS 0.176 [S2]. It surpasses Zip-NeRF in SSIM (0.828) and LPIPS (0.189) on this dataset [S2]. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), Mini-Splatting-D training time is 31m48s, Peak GPU Memory is 7.45GB, Rendering FPS is 83, Peak Rendering Memory is 3.12GB [S2].
+A quality-prioritized variant of Mini-Splatting that omits the simplification process to maximize rendering quality [S2]. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), Mini-Splatting-D uses 4.69M Gaussians, achieving SSIM 0.831, PSNR 27.51, LPIPS 0.176. It surpasses Zip-NeRF in SSIM (0.828) and LPIPS (0.189) on this dataset [S2]. Training time is 31m48s, Peak GPU Memory is 7.45GB, Rendering FPS is 83, Peak Rendering Memory is 3.12GB [S2].
 
 ### Mini-Splatting-C
-Designed for scenarios requiring storage compression [S2]. This pipeline is directly based on Mini-Splatting [S2]. Given a pre-trained Mini-Splatting model, it stores the Gaussian centers as float32, while other attributes, such as scales and SH coefficients, are processed using transform coding and a standard zip compression [S2].
+Designed for scenarios requiring storage compression, this pipeline is directly based on Mini-Splatting. Given a pre-trained Mini-Splatting model, it stores Gaussian centers as float32, while other attributes (scales, SH coefficients) are processed using transform coding and standard zip compression [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Training Time:** Mini-Splatting reduces training time significantly [S2]. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), it takes 17m56s compared to 30m8s for standard 3DGS [S2].
-*   **Peak GPU Memory:** Mini-Splatting reduces peak GPU memory usage [S2]. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), it uses 2.61GB compared to 7.45GB for standard 3DGS [S2].
-*   **Rendering FPS:** Mini-Splatting increases rendering speed [S2]. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), it achieves 410 FPS compared to 98 FPS for standard 3DGS [S2].
-*   **Peak Rendering Memory:** Mini-Splatting reduces peak rendering memory [S2]. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), it uses 0.40GB compared to 2.79GB for standard 3DGS [S2].
+*   **Training Time:** Mini-Splatting reduces training time significantly. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), it takes 17m56s compared to 30m8s for vanilla 3DGS [S2].
+*   **Peak GPU Memory:** Reduced from 7.45GB (3DGS) to 2.61GB (Mini-Splatting) on Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor) [S2].
+*   **Rendering FPS:** Increases from 98 FPS (3DGS) to 410 FPS (Mini-Splatting) on Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor) [S2].
+*   **Peak Rendering Memory:** Reduced from 2.79GB (3DGS) to 0.40GB (Mini-Splatting) [S2].
 
 **Measured Performance:**
-*   **Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor):** Mini-Splatting uses 0.49M Gaussians vs 3.36M for 3DGS (7x fewer), achieving SSIM 0.822 vs 0.815, PSNR 27.34 vs 27.21, LPIPS 0.217 vs 0.214 [S2].
-*   **MipNeRF360 (General):** Mini-Splatting achieves SSIM 0.763, PSNR 25.83, LPIPS 0.273, and a size of 18.2 MB [S3].
-*   **Model Size Reduction:** Mini-Splatting achieves up to 10x model size reduction compared to original 3DGS [S4].
+*   **Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor):** Mini-Splatting uses 0.49M Gaussians (7x fewer than 3DGS's 3.36M). Metrics: SSIM 0.822 (vs 0.815), PSNR 27.34 (vs 27.21), LPIPS 0.217 (vs 0.214) [S2].
+*   **Mip-NeRF360 (General):** Another variant achieves SSIM 0.763, PSNR 25.83, LPIPS 0.273, with a model size of 18.2 MB [S3].
+*   **Model Size:** Up to 10x reduction compared to original 3DGS [S4].
 
 **Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Significantly reduced memory footprint and faster training/rendering times while maintaining comparable or superior image quality metrics [S2]. The spatial reorganization approach avoids the artifacts associated with direct pruning [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** The use of fewer, larger Gaussians can lead to noticeable popping artifacts [S4]. The method requires careful tuning of the densification and simplification schedule [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient spatial distribution reduces redundancy; significant memory and speed improvements; comparable or better visual quality than dense 3DGS [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Fewer Gaussians lead to larger individual splats, increasing popping artifacts [S4]. The hard constraint requires careful balancing of densification and simplification schedules [S2].
 
 **When to Use:**
-*   Use Mini-Splatting when memory and compute resources are constrained, and high rendering speed is required [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   Use Mini-Splatting-D when rendering quality is prioritized over memory and speed [S2].
-*   Use Mini-Splatting-C when storage compression is the primary goal [S2].
+Use Mini-Splatting when memory bandwidth, training time, or rendering FPS are critical constraints, and a slight increase in popping artifacts is acceptable. Use Mini-Splatting-D when quality is prioritized over memory/speed constraints.
 
 ## Background
-Mini-Splatting was introduced by Fang and Wang in 2024 [S2]. It addresses the inefficiencies of vanilla 3DGS, specifically the non-uniform spatial distribution of Gaussians which leads to overlapping and under-reconstruction [S2]. The method formulates the optimization as a mixed-integer problem to bound memory footprints [S1, unofficial]. It is part of the broader effort to make 3D Gaussian Splatting more efficient for real-world applications [S1, unofficial][S2].
